    "narrative": "LawDog enters today\u2019s slate with **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** (80.87) and **Bryce Harper** (78.75) anchoring the lineup, representing our most potent scoring threats against favorable matchups. **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** benefits significantly from a soft matchup against Meaden Kelly (xERA 5.13), with his high-leverage spot in the two-hole providing ample RBI potential. Meanwhile, **Bryce Harper** draws an ideal scenario against Bradish; the combination of a positive park factor and a top-tier batting order position maximizes his output potential. **Lawrence Butler** (73.47) also demands attention, as a strong platoon advantage against K. Montero elevates him into our primary tier of reliable starters for today.\n\nThe most difficult decision on the bench was **Geraldo Perdomo** (44.54). While his profile is bolstered by a decent order spot, his efficiency score was dragged down by the matchup against G. Williams (xERA 2.99). We ultimately opted for the higher upside of **Masyn Winn** (64.31) and **Gleyber Torres** (67.01) to protect our scoring ceiling, as **Geraldo Perdomo** simply couldn\u2019t justify a start over the available platoon-favored bats. We remain cautious regarding **Dylan Beavers** (52.50); while he brings necessary speed to the lineup, he faces a daunting task against Wheeler (xERA 2.88). The combined negative modifiers\u2014specifically Wheeler's elite hold rate and catcher pop-time\u2014limit **Dylan Beavers**\u2019 stolen base probability, turning this into a high-risk, high-reward play that barely cleared our starting threshold. \n\nWe are monitoring **Cal Raleigh** (49.31) closely, as his score reflects a difficult matchup against T. Bradley. With his efficiency hovering near the marginal line, we are leaning on his power profile to provide value despite the projected struggles. Managers should keep an eye on **Yainer Diaz**, who is currently listed as a DNP/Bench; his absence from the lineup leaves us thin at catcher, placing the burden of production squarely on **Cal Raleigh**. Outside of these matchups, we have successfully mitigated risk by benching **Jung Hoo Lee** and **Drew Gilbert**, whose platoon-heavy disadvantages against L-handed starters like Hart necessitated their removal from the active rotation to preserve our game caps.",
